SALON / ADA, THIS TIME WITH A COMPILER 05 / ADA, THIS TIME WITH A COMPILER Ada. A woman and a language. One came before computers. The other reminds you that a variable's type is not an optional suggestion. begin end; A REAL PROGRAMMING LANGUAGE TEASCRIPT STAYS IN THE KETTLE A NAME IN TRIBUTE She did not write it. She inspired the name. The Ada language is named in honour of Ada Lovelace. It emerged from a US Department of Defense competition in the 1970s; Jean Ichbiah led the winning design. The first standard was published in 1983. Ada Lovelace lived from 1815 to 1852. She therefore neither created the language nor programmed in it. Its name honours her legacy. History of the language · AdaCore ↗ · Origin of the name · historical report in NASA NTRS, PDF ↗ Strong types. A soft spot for order. Three reasons why a compiler does not have to be your enemy. 01 / TYPES Tea is not an integer. A strong type system helps detect misuse of data. Many errors are caught at compile time. 02 / READABILITY Code is for reading too. The language favours explicit intent and readability. It takes words like begin and end seriously. 03 / RELIABILITY More than a pretty demo. Ada is used in embedded and real-time systems, including aviation and railways. The language alone does not guarantee bug-free software. Features and applications · AdaCore ↗ A LITTLE TASTE OF SYNTAX REAL CODE / ILLUSTRATIVE OUTPUT Hello, world. With well-behaved types. Choose one of three short examples. We will explain what it means and the output it would produce. EXAMPLE Greetings from the salon Three cups in a loop A type with a range Put_Line prints text and ends the line. The Hello procedure is the entry point of this example. Show expected output ↗ Copy code This browser example displays prepared expected output. It is not an Ada compiler and does not execute the code here. hello.adb ADA / EXAMPLE with Ada.Text_IO; use Ada.Text_IO;

procedure Hello is
begin
   Put_Line ("Hello, salon!");
end Hello; EXPECTED OUTPUT Select “Show expected output”. FROM YOUR FIRST LINE TO BETTER QUESTIONS Where next? 01 Learn the basics AdaCore's free Introduction to Ada course. Syntax, types, modules and concurrency. Open the course ↗ 02 Read the rules The Ada Reference Manual is an authoritative description of the language. Fewer jokes, more precision. Open the reference manuals ↗ 03 Meet SPARK SPARK is a language based on a subset of Ada that enables formal verification of program properties. Introduction to SPARK ↗ What does Ada 2022 mean? It is the name of the current approved edition of the standard according to the Ada Rapporteur Group; ISO approval took place in 2023. The next version in development is called Ada 202Y. Status checked on 11 November 2026. Source ↗ CONTINUE THROUGH THE SALON Enough types for now. Test your knowledge of Ada. ↗
